<Base title="Pressure washer hose ends" description="A replacement hose has two ends that may not match. Check M22 stem size or quick-connect body on each end before you click buy." path="/hose-ends/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Hose ends</p>
    <h1>Hose ends are two fittings</h1>
    <Answer>
      Read both ends. “M22 × M22” on a listing still hides 14 vs 15. “M22 × 3/8″ QC” is a different hose from “M22 × M22.”
    </Answer>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Listing language</th><th>What to verify</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>M22 × M22</td><td>Stem size both ends. 14×14, 15×15, or mixed (rare, usually a mistake).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>M22 × QC</td><td>Which M22 stem, and whether the QC is 1/4″ or 3/8″.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3/8″ QC × 3/8″ QC</td><td>Easiest long-term hose if you already converted the pump and gun.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Kärcher K hose</td><td>Clip profile, not M22. See <a href="/brands/karcher/">Kärcher</a>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Hose <em>ID</em> (1/4″ vs 5/16″ vs 3/8″) is a flow choice. It does not tell you the nut size. A 3/8″ ID hose can still wear M22-14 mm nuts.</p>
    <p>Next: <a href="/replacement-hose/">buying a replacement</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
